  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 348 AM CDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\n08z/2am radar imagery shows a large cluster of convection along\nthe Illinois/Wisconsin border. The storms are occurring ahead of\na weak area of low pressure analyzed over northern Iowa and are\nbeing fed by a 30-40kt 850mb jet streak oriented from the\nCentral Plains to the southern Great Lakes. The convection will\ncontinue to track E/SE across northern Illinois and will remain\nlargely north of the KILX CWA this morning. Have featured 30-60\nPoPs along/north of I-74 through midday in case some of the storms\nare able to drop/develop that far south as the main cluster \npushes into northern Indiana. \n\nA prominent outflow boundary is currently evident from near \nWaukegan to Moline...and this feature will likely serve as a \nfocus for additional convective development this afternoon as it\nsags further southward. Most CAMs key in on locations along/east\nof a Champaign to Shelbyville line after 20z/3pm. While the\natmosphere will be moderately unstable with SBCAPEs greater than\n3000J/kg, weak 0-6km wind shear of less than 30kt will inhibit\nwidespread storm organization. As a result, am only expecting\nisolated marginally severe wind gusts of 50-60mph with the\nstrongest cells.\n\nAs the nocturnal low-level jet shifts a bit further southward\ntonight, showers and thunderstorms will become more widespread\nacross the N/NW CWA after dark. Based on the latest model\nguidance, have opted to carry 60-80 PoPs along/north of a\nRushville to Normal line. Further southeast, locations along and\nsouth of the I-70 corridor will remain dry through the entire\nnight.  \n\nWith a short-wave trough interacting with a stationary frontal \nboundary draped across the region, showers and thunderstorms\nwill continue into Sunday. While instability will be slightly\nlower than today, an uptick in wind shear associated with the\nwave will lead to a somewhat better chance for strong to\npotentially severe storms during peak heating. NAM SBCAPEs reach\n2000-2500J/kg while 0-6km bulk shear increases to around 30k by\nafternoon. Given these parameters and a consistent reflectivity\nsignal noted by the RRFS, think some of the storms late Sunday\nafternoon and evening will be capable of marginally severe wind\ngusts/hail. The latest SPC Day 2 Convective Outlook highlights\nareas along/south of a Rushville to Bloomington line for a\nMarginal Risk (level 1 of 5) for severe weather. This seems\nreasonable, although it may need to be nudged northward a bit if\nRRFS trends continue.\n\nWet weather will continue on Monday and even into Tuesday as an\nupper low evident on latest water vapor imagery over Idaho\ngradually traverses the region. Once the low passes to the east,\nsynoptic subsidence will lead to a return to dry weather for\nthe remainder of next week. The big story will be the cooler\ntemperatures as highs drop into the 60s north of the I-70\ncorridor Monday and Tuesday, then only slowly rise back into the\n70s as the week progresses. The coolest period continues to be\nTuesday night when lows reach the lower to middle 50s. A few \nfavored low-lying spots may even dip into the upper 40s as the\n00z NBM indicates a 30-50% chance of temps below 50 degrees by\n12z Wednesday.",
